# Pathophysiology and Pharmacology
#
# Drug classes and syndromes at the level of mechanism, with ordered therapies kept apart from nursing actions and every guideline named with its year; no doses, targets, or protocols.

#>nursing_pathophysiology
#:layer #!reference
#:domain #!nursing
#:form #!ledger
#:level #!advanced
#:review #!adversarial_checked
#:valence #!bane

^"emit"{
 ~#title: "Pathophysiology and Pharmacology"
 ~#claim: "Knowing why a drug works and when it harms lets a nurse watch for the right signs; the order, the dose, and the protocol belong to the prescriber and the current guideline."
 ~#proof: "Mechanisms from Goodman and Gilman, 14th edition; vancomycin monitoring from the 2020 ASHP and IDSA consensus; ACS from the 2025 ACC and AHA guideline; DKA from the 2024 ADA and EASD consensus."
 ~#door: "Read orders and actions first, then one syndrome at a time, checking each guideline in the currency ledger."
 ~#audience: "nursing students who have finished clinical foundations"
 continuity: "Pathophysiology and Pharmacology | why a drug works"
 cold_open: "Knowing why a drug works and when it harms tells a nurse what to watch for at the bedside."
}

